content:
`prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` is an OS-level user setting, most often enabled by people with vestibular disorders, migraine triggers, or motion sensitivity. Ignoring it can cause genuine nausea and dizziness. It must be handled at system level, not component by component.

THE POLICY, IN FOUR RULES:
- DISABLE: remove sliding, scaling, rotation, parallax, and any large-area movement. These are the vestibular triggers.
- REPLACE: substitute an instant state change or a simple opacity fade. Opacity transitions are generally acceptable under reduced motion.
- PRESERVE: keep animation that conveys essential state — loading spinners, progress bars, sync indicators. Removing these removes information.
- TOKEN APPROACH: override every duration token to ~0.01ms inside a single `prefers-reduced-motion` block at `:root`. One rule, whole system covered:

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  :root { --duration-instant:0.01ms; --duration-fast:0.01ms; --duration-normal:0.01ms;
          --duration-moderate:0.01ms; --duration-slow:0.01ms; --duration-deliberate:0.01ms; }
  *, *::before, *::after { animation-iteration-count:1 !important; scroll-behavior:auto !important; }
}
```
(0.01ms rather than 0 so `transitionend` handlers still fire.)

IN FIGMA: there is no reduced-motion mode in prototypes. Express it as a MOTION VARIABLE MODE in Figma Motion — a 'reduced' mode where duration and easing variables flatten — and name that mode explicitly in handoff.

WHAT DESIGNERS MUST STILL DECIDE: which animations carry information (preserve) and which are decorative (remove). Engineering cannot make that call, and a blanket kill-switch on all motion removes loading feedback along with the parallax.
